Subject: [PATCH] gl: allow the SwiftShader WebGL fallback by default

The AllowSwiftShaderFallback feature is enabled by default. On a host
with no usable GPU, such as a headed browser under Xvfb with Mesa
llvmpipe, canvas.getContext("webgl") returns a SwiftShader context
instead of null. Almost every real desktop browser has WebGL, so a null
context identifies the browser more surely than any renderer string. The
page console gets no deprecation warning about the fallback, and
--disable-features=AllowSwiftShaderFallback turns it off.

--- a/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
@@ -318,9 +318,40 @@
 }
 #endif
 
-// Allow fallback to SwfitShader without command line flags during the
-// deprecation period.
-BASE_FEATURE(kAllowSwiftShaderFallback, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+// Allow fallback to SwiftShader without command line flags.
+//
+// Upstream deprecated this from Chrome 130 and removed it on Linux and macOS
+// from Chrome 139, leaving the default disabled, so on a host with no usable
+// GPU driver canvas.getContext("webgl") now returns null instead of degrading
+// to software. Measured on a headless Linux server under Xvfb, where the only
+// driver is Mesa llvmpipe and the blocklist disables WebGL for it:
+// context_group.cc reports ContextResult::kFatalFailure "WebGL1 blocklisted",
+// chrome://gpu reports WebGL disabled, and a page gets null.
+//
+// Re-enabled here because that null is the strongest tell on the surface this
+// fork exists to serve. Effectively no real desktop browser lacks WebGL, so
+// absence identifies the browser more surely than any renderer string it could
+// have carried -- and with no context, the composed GPU identity, the served
+// limits and the served extension list are all unreachable. It lands hardest
+// on headed-under-a-virtual-display, which is this project's recommended
+// maximum-stealth configuration.
+//
+// This ALLOWS the fallback, it does not force it: selection still prefers
+// hardware, and IsSwiftShaderAllowed() is consulted only once hardware has
+// failed. A host with a working GPU reaches the same branch it reached before.
+//
+// A feature default rather than appending --enable-unsafe-swiftshader during
+// startup, for three reasons. It needs no propagation to the GPU process,
+// where the decision is also made. It leaves no argv footprint -- a command
+// line is readable by any local process, and a browser advertising
+// --enable-unsafe-swiftshader in its own argv is a tell of a different kind.
+// And it cannot be keyed on patch 0091's GPU-less detection, which is one-way
+// by design: the reference deployment has /dev/dri/renderD128 present with no
+// usable driver behind it, so that probe reports a hardware backend and would
+// miss precisely this case.
+//
+// --disable-features=AllowSwiftShaderFallback remains the way to turn it off.
+BASE_FEATURE(kAllowSwiftShaderFallback, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
 bool IsSwiftShaderAllowedByFeature() {
   return base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kAllowSwiftShaderFallback);
--- a/gpu/command_buffer/service/gles2_cmd_decoder_passthrough.cc
+++ b/gpu/command_buffer/service/gles2_cmd_decoder_passthrough.cc
